    "q-108750": {
      "dom_id": "step-q108750",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+8y-1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+4y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2(x+4y)-1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+4y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1) & =\\frac{2z-1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2z.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
