    "q-108819": {
      "dom_id": "step-q108819",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+5y)-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=10x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5(x+y))-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z).\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =2x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =10x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)|=10x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y).\n$$",
