    "q-149760": {
      "dom_id": "step-q149760",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2+4{x}^{2}-4xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2+(2x-y)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2+{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}={z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{z} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2x-y}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
